+// browser: records one tab to a fragmented MP4 at a constant frame rate, for
+// the Browser.*VideoRecording commands. The tab is captured through
+// WebContentsVideoCaptureDevice, as tab capture does, at a fixed size (the
+// capturer letterboxes a page of another shape), so the window is never
+// resized and the page sees nothing. Frames go to AgentVideoEncodingService
+// in a sandboxed utility process, which paces, encodes and muxes them and
+// streams the file's bytes back; this side appends them to the file and
+// keeps the events sidecar, both on a blocking sequence. The service also
+// gets the GPU process's encoder provider, unless the client asked for
+// software, and says at its start which H.264 encoder runs.
+//
+// One recording at a time in the browser, held by the Registry with the
+// session that started it: that session gets the recording's end event, and
+// its detach ends the recording.
+//
+// The media clock: zero half a sample after the first frame's reference
+// time (the moment the page shows it), stopped while paused, and moved by at
+// most half the page's frame spacing to keep the file's ticks between the
+// page's frames (AlignTicks). Every frame goes to the service with its time
+// on this clock, so it is the file's own time: the service stamps sample n at
+// n/fps s on the same clock (agent_video_encoding_service_impl.cc), and each
+// fragment's tfdt carries those stamps, so marks and pauses report it as is.
+// The muxer truncates single sample durations to whole track units, which
+// tfdt corrects at the next fragment.
+//
+// The page sees nothing: the tab is captured as a hidden capturer
+// (WebContentsVideoCaptureDevice::KeepPageHidden), so its visibility and
+// size hints never change, and no capture scale override is asked for.
+//
+// The CDP cursor (CdpCursorOverlay) is in the frames unless the client
+// turned it off: viz blends it in as a capture overlay, which this side
+// reads from the cursor and sends at most once per frame period. The
+// windowing system's cursor never is.
+//
+// The tab's sound goes into the same file unless the client turned it off:
+// each capture also takes a loopback of its tab's audio (AgentAudioCapture),
+// and each buffer goes to the service with its place on the media clock,
+// from its capture time, which is its playout time on the same clock as a
+// frame's reference time. The service encodes it (AAC through the GPU
+// process's encoder, else Opus) and muxes it beside the frames. A failure of
+// the sound, here or in the service, never ends the recording: the file goes
+// on without sound and the sidecar says audioLost.
